## The judging loop

```
read run-{NN} --unread
  -> drain the VERIFY queue FIFO
  -> judge each against its NAMED LEG in RUN.md (not against the builder's narrative)
  -> post VERIFY-PASS or VERIFY-BOUNCE (bounce carries the failing evidence and a `SCOPE:` line —
     "fix this row only"; a row bounced TWICE is BLOCKED and the third body goes to the order's author)
  -> THEN, and only then, run your own independent checks (browser / artifact / query)
```

Verification is first. Your own checks are second. A `VERIFY` sitting unread while you check is
the failure this ordering exists to prevent.

**Judge the leg, not the report.** You do not accept "tests pass" or "I committed the fix" as
evidence. You go to ground truth yourself: the artifact, the browser, the query. Bounces are healthy — a run with zero bounces and zero independent checks is a run with
no gate.

```bash
python .claude/comms/comms.py read run-{NN} lane-{last} --unread
python .claude/comms/comms.py post run-{NN} lane-{last} --to lane-a --wo <row> \
  --subject "VERIFY-BOUNCE <row>: <what failed>" "<the failing evidence, verbatim>"
python .claude/comms/comms.py post run-{NN} lane-{last} --to lane-b --wo <row> \
  --subject "WATCH <row>: <the instruction>" "<why>"
```

Your own commits — which touch **only** `runs/run-{NN}/**` and backlog files — carry the marker
`[run-{NN}][lane-{last}][<row-id>] <subject>` on the first line, same format as the builders.
The conformance check reads that marker to prove you built nothing: it lists every path in
every commit carrying it and fails if any path falls outside `runs/` or `backlog/`. A stray
product-code path in one of your commits is how a gate stops being a gate.

`WATCH` is yours alone. It is obeyed, not debated. Use it when you observe drift or a hazard —
a lane writing outside territory, a lane re-verifying something in the do-not-re-verify list, a
lane about to touch a shared hot file.

